    "result_text": "Done. The full build is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**`LocalityResolver.java`** \u2014 added a postcode-first overload `localityOf(String postcode, String city)`:\n- It first tries to place the postcode in a known region range (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099) via a new `REGION_RANGE` table.\n- Only if the postcode is absent, blank, non-numeric, or in no known range does it fall back to the existing city-to-region table.\n- The original single-arg `localityOf(String city)` is kept intact (still used by `Postcode` validation, whose semantics are unchanged).\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `locality` mapping now passes both fields: `LocalityResolver.localityOf(owner.getPostcode(), owner.getCity())`.\n\n## Why this satisfies the spec\n- **Known cities return the same region**: e.g. Sydney + postcode 2000 \u2192 NSW (postcode and city agree).\n- **Disambiguation**: an unknown/random city with postcode 3000 \u2192 VIC, because the postcode wins over the city table \u2014 exactly what `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity` asserts.\n- **Backward compatibility**: an owner with no postcode falls back to the city table, and one with neither still yields `UNKNOWN`.\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest.coreRegionFromPostcodeRange` and `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity` both pass, and the full `./mvnw test` build succeeds (2/2, 0 failures).",
